From: "Orsavio P. Delmare" <orsavio@delmare.lu>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 13:09:05 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 50/50] README: make this describe our EnGenius tree, not the
 upstream project

The README was inherited from the fork source and never rewritten, so this
repo was advertising someone else's project to anyone who landed on it.

Concretely, it opened by describing Xiaomi/Redmi/Linksys/GL.iNet routers,
sent readers to a different account's prebuilt-images page and wiki, gave
build instructions that clone a different repo on a branch this tree does
not have (nss-edma-rework; ours is engenius-ipq807x), and carried a Support
section soliciting donations to another person's GitHub Sponsors and PayPal.
It also predated the ECW336 entirely and still claimed the EnGenius support
was three AP-HK07 SKUs with a 2.5GbE QCA8081 uplink.

Rewritten around what this tree actually is: four EnGenius IPQ807x APs,
including the ECW336, which has no OpenWrt support anywhere else. The board
table now carries each SKU's product_id, board design, radios, uplink and --
importantly -- whether anyone has actually booted it. Only the ECW336 has,
and the README says so rather than implying four supported devices.

Added the board-identity section, because the labels actively mislead: the
ECW336's stock DTB sits in the slot named hk07 and carries
model = "...AP-HK07", yet its compatible is qcom,ipq8074-ap-hk01-c6; the
stock kernel is a ~25-DTB multi-board blob whose FIT default points at
config@hk08, matching neither; and machid is a slot selector rather than a
product ID, since an EWS377-FIT reports the same value. Trust compatible.

Added the install cautions that matter on this hardware: write the inactive
A/B slot, keep UART attached, never erase or rebuild the bootloader env
(the ECW336's only real MAC lives there), treat 0:ART as read-only, and
check /proc/mtd on an ECW230v3 because units have been seen with the
rootfs/rootfs_1 labels reversed. Kept the stale-kmod sk_buff warning, since
that trap cost a full session and looks exactly like an NSS bug.

Acknowledgements kept and extended -- this tree genuinely stands on the
nss-edma tree and nss-packages feed it forked, plus Ansuel's EDMA/PPE work,
robimarko's qualcommax maintenance, qosmio's NSS packaging, and Qualcomm/
CodeLinaro's host components. The feed URL stays because the build really
does depend on it. What is gone is the Support section: this repo should not
solicit money on anyone's behalf. GPL-2.0 requires preserving copyright
notices in the source, which is untouched; a README credits list and a
donation link are not license obligations.
